Stop yes when any write fails, not only the newline's.
yes checked only the result of the putchar that ends each line. A
buffered stream reports a failed write only to the call that flushes
the buffer, and some C libraries (msvcrt) then empty the buffer and
accept further output. Unless that call was the newline, which for
many line lengths it never is, the failure went to the fputs or putchar
that writes the text, whose result was ignored, so yes never noticed a
write error, such as standard output open read-only or a full disk,
and ran forever. Test the stream's error indicator after each line
instead.

---
 coreutils/yes.c |    3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/coreutils/yes.c
+++ b/coreutils/yes.c
@@ -48,7 +48,8 @@
 				break;
 			putchar(' ');
 		}
-	} while (putchar('\n') != EOF);
+		putchar('\n');
+	} while (!ferror(stdout));
 
 	bb_perror_nomsg_and_die();
 }
